---
title: "Working with INI config files using stdlib/ini"
author: contributor
date: 2026-11-21
abstract: A hands-on tutorial for the stdlib ini module: parse INI text into a config map, read strings, ints, floats and bools with defaults, update values, and write the config back out. Includes a runnable example with a pinned test vector.

# Why INI, why this module

INI is the config format that refuses to die: sections in brackets, `key = value` lines, comments starting with `;` or `#`. The stdlib `ini` module (`stdlib/ini.ltl`) parses that text into a plain map — `map[section -> map[key -> value]]` — and gives you typed getters on top, so a port number comes back as an int and a debug flag as a bool instead of everything being a string you convert by hand.

This tutorial walks through the whole loop: parse, read, update, serialise. Everything below is exercised by the companion example, `examples/ini_tutorial.ltl`, which runs as-is and asserts every expected value.

# The pinned test vector

One fixed document is used throughout the tutorial, so every result can be checked by eye:

    ; a comment
    [server]
    host = example.com
    port = 8080
    debug = true
    timeout = 2.5

    [ui]
    title = "My App"

# Parsing and reading values

`parse(text)` returns the config map. The `get` family reads from it, and every getter takes a default that is returned when the section or key is missing:

fn main() {
    let cfg = parse(doc)
    get(cfg, "server", "host", "none")            // "example.com"
    get_int(cfg, "server", "port", 0)             // 8080
    get_bool(cfg, "server", "debug", false)       // true
    get_float(cfg, "server", "timeout", 0.0)      // 2.5
    get(cfg, "ui", "title", "none")               // "My App"
    get(cfg, "server", "missing", "fallback")     // "fallback"
}

Things worth knowing, all verified against the module as shipped:

- Quoted values are unquoted: `title = "My App"` reads back as `My App`.
- Lines starting with `;` or `#` are comments.
- The parser always creates a `default` section for keys that appear before any `[section]` header, so `sections(cfg)` returns `["default", "server", "ui"]` for the document above.
- `has_section(cfg, name)` and `has_key(cfg, section, key)` test for presence when a default is not enough.

# Updating and writing back

`set(cfg, section, key, value)` returns the updated config, and `to_string(cfg)` serialises a config back to INI text:

    let updated = set(cfg, "server", "host", "example.org")
    get(updated, "server", "host", "none")        // "example.org"
    print(to_string(updated))

Two honest quirks of the current serialiser: keys are emitted in alphabetical order within each section, and values are written in their runtime form, so the bool above comes back out as `debug = True`. The round trip is safe for reading back with this same module; do not expect byte-identical output.

`merge(base, other)` combines two configs, `delete(cfg, section, key)` removes a key, and `section_keys(cfg, section)` lists a section's keys.

# Running the example

`examples/ini_tutorial.ltl` is self-contained and runnable:

    lateralus run examples/ini_tutorial.ltl
    // ini tutorial: all checks passed

Like `examples/spiral_ranking.ltl`, the example inlines the modules it uses (`stdlib/strings.ltl` and `stdlib/ini.ltl`, verbatim) because `import stdlib.*` does not resolve in the runner yet. It also documents one gap it works around: `stdlib/ini.ltl` calls `substr(s, start, length)`, which the stdlib does not define for strings, so the example provides it in one line on top of the runtime's `slice()` builtin. If the module grows a native `substr`, the shim can simply be deleted.

Every line of the pinned test vector is asserted with `assert_eq`, so if the parser's behaviour changes, the example fails loudly instead of silently teaching stale behaviour — which is what a tutorial example is for.

  </script>
